1.	Introduction
Sound is a vibration that travels through a medium, such as air, as a pressure wave. The ear detects these vibrations and the brain interprets them as sound. 
A tone is a sound with a discernible pitch, associated with its frequency. Music organises sounds through rhythm, melody and harmony. 
Rhythm is the pattern of sounds and silences over time; harmony comes from combining different pitches, whether they sound consonant or dissonant.

This project will explore how computers can generate sound waves for a range of applications, from music to environmental sounds. 
It will begin by generating fixed pieces of sound in code and may develop into a console for playing sounds in real time. 
As a learning project, it will examine how software produces digital audio and how a computer’s hardware converts that audio into sound.

A longer-term ambition is to explore whether ideas from quantum physics can be represented or explained through musical structures. 
This will be treated as an exploratory direction, distinct from the project’s core work on sound synthesis.

